Chapter 9 361
Figure 9.18 – Cloud security posture management
One of the cornerstones of CSPM is Azure Policy, so let’s start with this.
Working with Azure Policy in practice
We previously introduced Azure Policy in Chapter 2, Solution Architecture. As a reminder, Azure
Policy is a foundational component of compliance, continuously evaluating your environment
and proactively blocking or identifying non-compliant deployments. Policies are defined with
specific effects—such as Audit, Deny, or DeployIfNotExists (see https://learn.microsoft.
com/en-us/azure/governance/policy/concepts/effect-basics for the complete list)—and
are applied to a chosen scope, such as a management group or subscription. Concrete examples
of policies could be the following:
• Ensuring that subnets have a default network security group attached
• Ensuring that Azure Storage and Azure SQL have public access denied
• Ensuring that encryption in transit is enabled for services where it’s still possible to ex -
pose port 80 (HTTP)
All of these rules, with a Deny effect, would prevent any non-compliant workload from being
deployed.
Security Architecture 362
The following XML snippet is an example of a built-in policy that ensures Azure SQL servers have
public access disabled:
"policyRule": {
"if": {
"allOf": [
{
"field": "type",
"equals": "Microsoft.Sql/servers"
},
{
"field": "Microsoft.Sql/servers/publicNetworkAccess",
"notEquals": "Disabled"
}
]
},
"then": {
"effect": "[parameters('effect')]",
"details": {
"conflictEffect": "audit",
"roleDefinitionIds": [
"/providers/Microsoft.Authorization/roleDefinitions/
6d8ee4ec-f05a-4a1d-8b00-a9b17e38b437"
],
"operations": [
{
"operation": "addOrReplace",
"field": "Microsoft.Sql/servers/publicNetworkAccess",
"value": "Disabled"
}
]
}
}
}
